      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The neo-noir movement that influenced **Noire** (2011) began in earnest with <a href=\"/movie/829\" class=\"movie-title\">Chinatown</a> (1974), which reimagined classic noir themes for the New Hollywood era. Films like **Body Heat** (1981) and **Blood Simple** (1984) followed, updating noir conventions while maintaining the genre's cynical worldview and moral ambiguity. **Noire** (2011) fits into this tradition while incorporating surrealist elements more commonly found in films like <a href=\"/movie/1018\" class=\"movie-title\">Mulholland Drive</a> (2001)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "What sets **Noire** (2011) apart is its unique visual approach. While most neo-noir films embrace color and modern cinematography, Chen deliberately shot in stark black and white using vintage lenses, creating a dreamlike quality that bridges classic noir like **Touch of Evil** (1958) with contemporary psychological thrillers like **Black Swan** (2010). SUBHEAD: Asian Noir Influences"
      },
